<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vyborg</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Viipuri, Viborg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vyborg, in Finnish Viipuri, is a port city at the head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a> on the Karelian Isthmus, now in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It grew around a castle founded by the Swedes in 1293, passed to Russia in 1710 and became part of the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a> in 1812. As one of Finland's largest cities it was ceded to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> in 1940, retaken in 1941 and finally lost in 1944, when its Finnish population was evacuated.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vyborg.jpg" alt="Vyborg" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: A.Savin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vyborg_June2012_View_from_Olaf_Tower_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Vyborg lies on the Karelian Isthmus near the head of Vyborg Bay, 130 kilometres north-west of St Petersburg and 38 kilometres south of the Russian border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. Its Swedish name Viborg may be interpreted as &quot;holy fort&quot;, from vi, &quot;sacred&quot;, and borg, &quot;fortress&quot; or &quot;city&quot;; the Finnish Viipuri adapts it. The area was a trading centre on the western branch of the Vuoksi River, since dried up, and was inhabited by Karelians, a Balto-Finnic people who came under the domination of Novgorod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Wider settlement is generally dated from the 13th century, when Hanseatic traders began travelling to Novgorod.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Vyborg Castle was founded in 1293 during the Third Swedish Crusade by the marshal Torkel Knutsson, on the site of an older Karelian fort that was burned. It was the first centre for the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karelia">Karelia</a> and was fought over for decades by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> and the Republic of Novgorod. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-noteborg">Treaty of Nöteborg</a> in 1323 recognised it as Swedish. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eric-of-pomerania">Eric of Pomerania</a> chartered its trade privileges in 1403, and it withstood a long siege in the Russo-Swedish War of 1496 to 1497.</p><p>Tsar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> captured the town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-northern-war">Great Northern War</a> in 1710, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nystad">Treaty of Nystad</a> of 1721 confirmed the transfer. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a> developed Fredrikshamn as a substitute port, and Vyborg's diocese moved to Borgå. The naval Battle of Vyborg Bay was fought on 4 July 1790. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> incorporated the town into the Grand Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>. The Saimaa Canal, opened in 1856, linked eastern Finland to the sea and benefited its trade. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-lenin">Vladimir Lenin</a> lived in the town for a period in 1917.</p><p>After Finnish independence, the town was held by the Red Guards until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-movement">White Guard</a> captured it on 29 April 1918; between 360 and 420 civilians were killed in the Vyborg massacre. In 1939 its population was slightly under 75,000, with minorities of Swedes, Germans, Russians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romani-people">Romani</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Alvar Aalto built the Vyborg Library there. Over 70,000 people were evacuated during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/winter-war">Winter War</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-peace-treaty">Moscow Peace Treaty</a> gave the town to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>; about 10,000 remaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> were evacuated in haste, so that practically the whole Finnish population was resettled elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Vyborg is the administrative centre of Vyborgsky District in Leningrad Oblast, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. The 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> recorded 72,530 inhabitants. The town lies 245 kilometres east of Helsinki, near the point where the Saimaa Canal enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-finland">Gulf of Finland</a>, and in 2010 it was given the status of City of Military Glory.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vyborg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Medieval Swedish warfare was directed east: expeditions traditionally called the Swedish crusades extended rule over Finland, Torgils Knutsson founded <b>Vyborg</b> castle in 1293, and the Treaty of Nöteborg of 1323 fixed a first border with Novgorod.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Moscow Peace Treaty of March 1940 ceded <b>Viipuri</b> and much of Karelia, about a tenth of the country, from which over 400,000 people were evacuated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-finland">Republic of Finland · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Near <b>Viborg</b> in Jutland, Valdemar I defeated his rival for the Danish throne, Sweyn III, who was killed while fleeing.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-23">23 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reds controlled the cities and industrial centres of southern Finland, including Helsinki, Tampere, Turku and <b>Viipuri</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/finnish-socialist-workers-republic">Finnish Socialist Workers' Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>Sweden pressed from the west and founded <b>Vyborg</b> in 1293.</blockquote><a href="/state/karelians">Karelians</a></li></ul></section>
